{"name":"Flying Fish","hex":"#5376ab","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"flying fish","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A tropical marine fish of the family Exocoetidae, having enlarged winglike pectoral fins enabling it to escape predators by taking short gliding flights through the air.","Used other than figuratively or idiomatically: see flying,‎ fish."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/flying_fish"}],"entries":[{"title":"Flying fish","blocks":[{"text":"The Exocoetidae are a family of saltwater ray-finned fish in the order Beloniformes, known colloquially as flying fish or flying cod, with about 64 species in seven genera. While they do not \"fly\" in the same way a bird does, flying fish can make powerful leaps out of the water where their long, wing-like paired fins act as aerofoils to generate lift and enable prolonged gliding for considerable distances above the water surface. The main reason for this behavior is thought to be to escape from underwater predators, which include swordfish, mackerel, tuna, and marlin, among others, though their periods of flight expose them to attack by aerial predators such as frigatebirds."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/72/Sailfin_flyingfish.jpg/330px-Sailfin_flyingfish.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flying_fish","wikidata":"Q183686"},{"title":"Flying Fish (film)","blocks":[{"text":"Flying Fish is a 2011 anthology film directed by Sri Lankan filmmaker Sanjeewa Pushpakumara. It was produced with the financial support of the Hubert Bals Fund of the International Film Festival Rotterdam (IFFR). The film made its world premiere on 28 January 2011, as part of the Rotterdam festival's Tiger Awards Competition. The film has been noted for its political value, beautiful cinematography, long takes, and shocking violence."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/FLYING_FISH_-Teaser_Poster-5.jpg/330px-FLYING_FISH_-Teaser_Poster-5.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flying_Fish_(film)","wikidata":"Q3783004"},{"title":"Fish","blocks":[{"text":"A fish is an aquatic, anamniotic, gill-bearing vertebrate animal with a tough cranium to protect the brain, but lacking limbs with digits. Fish can be grouped into the more basal jawless fish and the more common jawed fish, the latter including all living cartilaginous and bony fish, as well as the extinct placoderms and acanthodians. Tetrapods are descended from fish, so in a break from the long tradition of grouping all fish into a single class (Pisces), modern phylogenetics views fish as a paraphyletic group that includes all vertebrates except tetrapods. To treat fish as a monophyletic group, one would have to classify tetrapods as a clade/subgroup of specialized fish, which would make \"fish\" and \"vertebrates\" synonyms. In English, the plural of \"fish\" is fish when referring to individuals and fishes when referring to species."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7f/Balantiocheilos_melanopterus_-_Karlsruhe_Zoo_02_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Balantiocheilos_melanopterus_-_Karlsruhe_Zoo_02_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fish","wikidata":"Q152"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e8/Flying_Fish_%28PSF%29.png/500px-Flying_Fish_%28PSF%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e8/Flying_Fish_%28PSF%29.png/1280px-Flying_Fish_%28PSF%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e8/Flying_Fish_%28PSF%29.png/500px-Flying_Fish_%28PSF%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Flying fish","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFlying_Fish_(PSF).png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Pink-wing_flying_fish.jpg/500px-Pink-wing_flying_fish.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Pink-wing_flying_fish.jpg/1280px-Pink-wing_flying_fish.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Pink-wing_flying_fish.jpg/500px-Pink-wing_flying_fish.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Flying fish taking off","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APink-wing_flying_fish.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Flying_fish_for_sale_-_Tokyo_area_Apr_11_2019_08-32_PM.jpeg/500px-Flying_fish_for_sale_-_Tokyo_area_Apr_11_2019_08-32_PM.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Flying_fish_for_sale_-_Tokyo_area_Apr_11_2019_08-32_PM.jpeg/1280px-Flying_fish_for_sale_-_Tokyo_area_Apr_11_2019_08-32_PM.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Flying_fish_for_sale_-_Tokyo_area_Apr_11_2019_08-32_PM.jpeg/500px-Flying_fish_for_sale_-_Tokyo_area_Apr_11_2019_08-32_PM.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Dried flying fish for sale in Tokyo","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFlying_fish_for_sale_-_Tokyo_area_Apr_11_2019_08-32_PM.jpeg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/86/Makizushi.jpg/500px-Makizushi.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/86/Ma